  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 249 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\n(Tuesday Night - Friday)\n\nThe pattern remains largely stagnant through the end of the work \nweek with our region sitting beneath quasi-zonal flow aloft. A weak \ncold front is forecast to approach parts of northeast Missouri late \nWednesday night before stalling out and lifting back north as a warm \nfront later in the week. There is a low chance (20-30%) of rain \nshowers and possibly a thunderstorm or two in the vicinity of this \ncold front, mainly across northeast Missouri and west-central \nIllinois. \n\nTemperatures are forecast to moderate through Thursday, with most \nlocations getting back into the low 80s to the southeast of the cold \nfront. At least slightly cooler highs (upper 70s to near 80 degrees) \nare expected on Friday, mainly due to increasing mid/high level \nclouds. \n\n\n(Friday Night - Sunday)\n\nEnsemble guidance is in reasonably good agreement that another \nmidlevel shortwave trough will move across the south-central Plains \ninto the mid-Mississippi Valley this weekend. There remains some \nsubtle timing differences, but the shortwave looks somewhat \nreminiscent of the one working its way across the region early this \nweek. The shortwave itself is not particularly strong or organized, \nand gradually weakens with time as it gets sheared out as it moves \ninto more confluent flow aloft. The best chances (30-50%) of light \nrain showers are on Saturday afternoon/night as a wing of relatively \nmodest low-level moisture convergence moves from southwest to \nnortheast. At this point in time, the rain looks to stay on the \nlight side with plenty of dry time this weekend. The chances of a \nwidespread, significant (0.50+\") rain looks very low. The latest \nLREF probabilities for 0.50+\" through this weekend are only in the \n10-25% range from north to south, and have been dropping over the \npast 24-36 hours. \n\nA mostly cloudy weekend is certainly expected regardless of how much \nrain falls, which should help keep low temperatures on the mild side \n(mid 50s to near 60 degrees). Highs each day are expected to be in \nthe low to mid 70s. While still several degrees cooler than mid/late \nweek, these values would still be about 10 degrees above normal for \nlate October.",
